00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.060
Hey Crazies.
This is about 1 kilogram of mass.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.980
Then again, so is this.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.420
These two objects are different sizes, different shapes,

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.520
made of different materials, and have
different purposes.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.620
So, when I say something like this is 1 kilogram,
what am I saying about it?

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.140
What the heck is mass?!

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.720
In old school physics, mass shows up in two
different places.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.480
Kicking it old school with my home-boy Newton.
He’s no fool and that’s totally proven.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.120
He’s got a rule that’s meant for computing,
a major uncool of a mass executing.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.280
What. Whatever.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.600
According to Newton’s second law, mass measures inertia,

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.660
an object’s ability to resist changes in motion.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.400
More mass means less acceleration.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.240
Anyone who’s ever tried to push a broken
down car knows exactly what I mean.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.000
The second place mass appears is gravity.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.660
Newton’s universal law of gravity, which is in no way universal,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.560
says two masses exert gravity on
each other over some distance.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:08.020
More mass in either object means
more gravity between them both.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.860
Both laws are aspects of something we call
classical or Newtonian mechanics,

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.420
for obvious reasons.
And it works really well most of the time.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.460
We know from a lot of videos on this channel
that Newtonian mechanics doesn’t explain

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.480
everything. It’s not fundamentally correct.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.140
However, to be clear,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:32.420
Newton’s laws are unbelievably accurate
for 99% of things in 99% of the universe.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.680
Most of the time, you’re good with Newton.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.480
But his laws don’t really explain what mass is,

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.400
just what effects it has,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.140
which isn’t very satisfying.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.040
The easiest definition I’ve heard is that
mass is the amount of stuff

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.280
something has inside it, but that’s kind
of vague.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.260
What stuff?!

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.640
Yeah, exactly.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.700
This is a really deep question and the deeper
questions we ask,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.540
the more that other 1% of 1% is going to become important.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.480
Let’s focus on that for a few minutes

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.880
and, maybe, we can make this definition a little more specific.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.020
The other 1% of 1% of stuff is governed by one of two types of physics:

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.680
Relativity and Quantum Mechanics.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.720
We’ll start with relativity because,
well, it’s easier.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.900
In relativity, Newton’s second law of motion
and his law of gravity

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.080
have been corrected to more closely match
the universe.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.180
But there’s a consequence to this correction.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.520
Like a lot of other things in relativity, mass is relative to the observer.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.800
That means it depends on who or what is measuring it.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.880
Yes, angry commenters. Relativistic mass is a real thing.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.860
It isn’t always useful and sometimes it’s unnecessarily confusing,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.180
but it’s still a valid way of looking at the world.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.660
No matter what a particle physicist might tell you.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.600
To understand this, we need to take a look
at the most famous equation of all time.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.580
E equals m c squared.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.360
This mass here is relativistic mass.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.120
Whether you’re trying to accelerate an object
or you’re weighing it on some kind of scale,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.060
you’re measuring relativistic mass.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.060
In other words, you’re measuring its complete energy content.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.240
That energy could: elastic, kinetic, thermal, whatever.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.100
If the object has more energy,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.560
it’s harder to accelerate,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.400
it’s affected more by gravity,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:20.160
and it generates more of its own gravity,
no matter what type of energy that is.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.180
But there’s one type of energy that usually
dominates.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:24.640
Rest energy!

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.960
The part of mass associated with rest energy is often called rest mass,

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.100
so the distribution usually looks something like this.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.400
It’s almost all rest mass,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.540
so we don’t have to distinguish most of the time.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:38.340
Why do we call it rest mass?

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.360
It’s the mass we’d measure if it were at rest
relative to us.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.460
Say an object starts to speed up.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:49.260
Because its kinetic energy increases,
so does its total mass, otherwise known as

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.280
relativistic mass,
but its rest mass stays the same.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.080
While rest mass usually dominates, it doesn’t
always.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.500
If something is small enough or moving fast
enough,

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.420
kinetic energy can noticeably contribute to
overall mass.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:07.520
There are even things that don’t have any
rest mass at all.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.440
In order for something to have rest mass,
it must have a rest frame.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.540
There must be a frame of reference where it
is at rest or stationary.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.840
Light doesn’t have one of those.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.080
Light is always traveling at the same speed
according to all observers everywhere.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:28.920
It has relativistic mass, but not rest mass.
All of its energy is kinetic energy.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.860
In fact, anything without rest mass is going to have
that same speed-weirdness as light.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:39.980
Gluons also have no rest mass,
so they also travel at the speed of light for all observers.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:43.120
Speaking of gluons, let’s take a look at quantum mechanics.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.550
Because we still haven’t quite explained everything.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:50.180
How is this rest energy stuff actually energy?
What is it?!

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.840
It can’t just be existence energy.
Photons and gluons exist and they don’t have it.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.840
It comes down to what type of stuff 
objects are made of.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.320
You mean steel?

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.900
No! Particles!

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.680
Steel is mostly iron and carbon atoms in a
crystal solid.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.540
So far, there’s nothing super enlightening.
Steel has mass because its atoms do.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.340
Taking a closer look at one of the atoms,
we have the same problem.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:16.200
Atoms have mass because their subatomic particles do

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.140
and most of that mass is from the protons
and neutrons.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.620
Inside one of the protons though, something
weird happens.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:27.000
Protons are made of three quarks,
but the mass of those quarks only represents

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.640
1% of the total mass of the proton.
The other 99% comes from the kinetic energy

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.870
of those quarks
and, more importantly, the binding energy

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:40.180
of the gluon field holding those quarks together
as a proton.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:44.480
On a fundamental level, 99% of the mass of
you or me,

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.620
what we would call our rest mass,
is actually just other types of energy.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.200
So the break down actually looks more like
this.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.780
Almost the entire chart is made up of different
types of energy.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.782
But what about this remaining 1%?
That’s just the rest mass of all of these

00:05:58.782 --> 00:06:00.140
particles over here.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:04.180
According to quantum field theory, those particles
have mass due to an interaction with

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:10.000
something called the Higgs field.
See, most quantum fields hover around zero energy.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.140
The Higgs field hovers a little higher than the others.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.720
Its equilibrium is a non-zero energy that fills all space.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.460
The gruesome details of this we'll save for another video in the distant future.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.300
The point I’m making here is that it’s still about energy.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.720
The Higgs field is an energy field.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.400
All quantum fields are.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.880
So, what the heck is mass?

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.420
When you measure the mass of something, anything,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:37.760
you’re just measuring its energy content.
That energy can be made up of a wide range

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.240
of different types,
but, in the end, it’s all just energy.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:47.240
And, just so I make this abundantly clear,
less than 1% of that is from the Higgs field.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:52.680
The other 99% or more is from other energy
that has nothing to do with Higgs.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:56.280
Whatever part of that you happen to be calling
mass just depends

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.000
on who are and who you’re talking to.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.020
But since this famous equation says that mass is really just energy,

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.960
maybe it’s time we stop discussing mass all together.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.100
So, what’s the most massive thing you’ve
ever seen?

